  "notes": "Tetum-Dili as written by Tatoli, GMN and the Timor Post: administrative vocabulary is almost entirely Portuguese-derived, so the instrument list is mostly loans, while the movement list mixes loans with native verbs (hasa'e, hatun, hamenus, hasai, hapara, fahe, fó sai, hametin, haluan, foti desizaun). Timorese press spelling is unstable on accents and on é/e, so accented and unaccented variants of the same word are both listed (subsídiu/subsidiu, izensaun/isensaun, hahú/hahu, anúnsia/anunsia). Timor-specific instruments are included: 'bolsa da mãe' (the cash transfer scheme), 'jornál repúblika' (the official gazette), 'orsamentu jerál estadu'. Roughly a dozen English source concepts have no settled Tetum rendering and were left out rather than invented: voucher/coupon, rebate, buffer stock, price freeze/congelamento, windfall tax, excise as distinct from consumption tax, bailout/recapitalisation, safety net as a fixed phrase, and 'tender' as a noun. Bare 'lei', 'taxa' and 'fó' were dropped as too common in ordinary prose; only their compounds are kept."
